# ERP CORE RULES

Three independent repositories:
1. cursor-node-api — the API. Only this talks to MySQL. All business logic and DB access live here. ERP-API is cursor-node-api; it owns the database.
2. cursor-admin-ui — the admin portal.
3. cursor-customer-portal — the customer website / storefront.

One central MySQL database.

ERP-API (cursor-node-api) is the single source of business logic and database access.

Frontend applications must never access MySQL directly. They call cursor-node-api only.

The system is multi-tenant.

Company-owned records must be isolated by tenant/company context.

Never trust a client-supplied company_id for authorization.

Modules must be separated by business domain.

Avoid giant files and tightly coupled modules.

Use:
routes -> controllers -> services -> repositories -> models/database

Frontend:
pages/components -> services -> API

Every financial operation must be auditable.

Every stock-changing operation must produce a traceable inventory ledger movement.

Do not change posted accounting history silently.

Do not change inventory silently.

Use migrations for schema changes.

Do not mark work complete without tests and documentation.

Before large implementation:
PLAN -> INSPECT -> DESIGN -> IMPLEMENT -> TEST -> REVIEW -> DOCUMENT
